vkd3d: an explicit frame rate cap must not depend on present_wait.

`VKD3D_FRAME_RATE` is read at swapchain init, sets `frame_rate_limit.enable`
and `has_user_override`, and announces itself:

    dxgi_vk_swap_chain_init_frame_rate_limiter:
      Set frame rate limit to 15.0 FPS via environment.

And then never runs. The function that actually sleeps is called from one
place, behind a gate that has nothing to do with the cap:

    if (chain->present.wait && !entry.present_timing_enabled)
        dxgi_vk_swap_chain_delay_next_frame(chain, end_frame_time_ns);

`chain->present.wait` is `presentWait || wait2`, and on this device it is
false -- the neighbouring FIXME fires in every session log:

    Implementation supports neither present_wait1 or present_wait2.
    Latency will increase.

So a cap the user asked for is configured, logged, and silently discarded.
Measured on device with `VKD3D_FRAME_RATE=15`: Requiem delivers **42 frames a
second**, and the D3D11 title in the same container with DXVK's independent
limiter and the same cap delivers exactly 15.0. That is the whole difference
between the two APIs stuttering differently, and it is one condition.

`delay_next_frame` is safe to call without present_wait. Its only use of the
flag is choosing `frame_latency` for the *heuristic*, which decides whether to
engage a limiter that was not explicitly requested -- and that branch is
skipped outright when `enable` is already true, which an explicit cap makes it.
Everything after that is the deadline arithmetic, which needs a completion time
and nothing else. Without present_wait the wait thread takes the
`drain_complete_semaphore` path instead, so `end_frame_time_ns` is still a real
present completion; it is a weaker instant than scanout, which is exactly what
the FIXME above is warning about, and it is what DXVK's limiter has always used
to pace this container's D3D11 titles correctly.

Gated on `has_user_override` rather than on `enable`, so this changes behaviour
only where the user set the variable. The heuristic path, which needs
present_wait to reason about buffering, is left exactly as it was.

diff --git a/libs/vkd3d/swapchain.c b/libs/vkd3d/swapchain.c
index 5f2e9355..58dad508 100644
--- a/libs/vkd3d/swapchain.c
+++ b/libs/vkd3d/swapchain.c
@@ -3659,7 +3659,19 @@ static void *dxgi_vk_swap_chain_wait_worker(void *chain_)
 
         end_frame_time_ns = vkd3d_get_current_time_ns();
 
-        if (chain->present.wait && !entry.present_timing_enabled)
+        /* An explicitly requested cap does not depend on present_wait.
+         *
+         * This gate used to be `chain->present.wait` alone, so on an
+         * implementation offering neither present_wait1 nor present_wait2 the
+         * limiter was initialised, announced in the log, and then never asked
+         * to sleep. delay_next_frame only consults present.wait to size the
+         * heuristic's frame latency, and the heuristic is skipped when the
+         * limiter is already enabled -- which an explicit VKD3D_FRAME_RATE
+         * makes it. Without present_wait the completion time is a drained
+         * semaphore rather than scanout, which is less precise and is what the
+         * FIXME at init already warns about. */
+        if ((chain->present.wait || chain->frame_rate_limit.has_user_override) &&
+                !entry.present_timing_enabled)
             dxgi_vk_swap_chain_delay_next_frame(chain, end_frame_time_ns);
 
         /* If we're rendering with IMMEDIATE, we ignore present timing. */
